===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fro|literal}}, from {{der|en|LL.|litteralis}}, also {{m|la|literalis||of or pertaining to letters or to writing}}, from {{der|en|la|littera}}, {{m|la|litera||a letter}}; see [[letter]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɪt(ə)ɹəl/}}<ref>http://www.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/english/literal</ref>
* {{homophones|en|littoral}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Mélange a trois-literal.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Exactly as [[state]]d; [[read]] or [[understand|understood]] without additional [[interpretation]]; [[accord]]ing to the [[letter]] or verbal expression; [[real]]; not [[figurative]] or [[metaphorical]].
#: ''The '''literal''' translation is “hands full of bananas” but it means empty-handed.''
#* {{rfdate|en|}} Hooker
#*: a middle course between the rigour of '''literal''' translation and the liberty of paraphrasts
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=January 12, 2017
|author=Jesse Hassenger
|title=A literal monster truck is far from the stupidest thing about Monster Trucks
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/review/literal-monster-truck-far-stupidest-thing-about-mo-248111
|page=
|passage=Mechanically, operating this hybrid vehicle is sort of a cross between driving a car and taming an animal, which means the movie treats the audience to the sight of a man (pretending to be a teenager) driving a '''literal''' monster truck in a field next to a woman (also pretending to be a teenager) riding a horse. }}
# [[follow|Following]] the letter or exact words; not [[free]]; not taking [[liberties]].
#: ''A '''literal''' reading of the law would prohibit it, but that is clearly not the intent.''
# {{lb|en|uncommon}} Consisting of, or expressed by, [[letter]]s.
#: ''a '''literal''' equation''
#* {{rfdate|en|}} Johnson
#*: The '''literal''' notation of numbers was known to Europeans before the ciphers.
# {{lb|en|of a person}} Giving a strict or literal construction; unimaginative; matter-of-fact.
# {{lb|en|proscribed}} {{non-gloss|Used non-literally as an intensifier; see [[literally]] for usage notes.}}
#: ''Telemarketers are the '''literal''' worst.''

